#!/usr/bin/env zsh
# clicue — prototype cue card. Remove this file to disable entirely.
#
# Loads after 40-plugins so it registers its ZLE hooks AFTER zsh-autosuggestions,
# letting it append its card below whatever autosuggestions puts in POSTDISPLAY
# rather than overwriting it.
#
# NOTE: all zstyles must be set BEFORE the source line — keys are bound at
# source time from this configuration.

# ── behaviour ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
zstyle ':clicue:*' min-input 1        # chars before the card appears
zstyle ':clicue:*' tier1-rows 10      # cues in the primary card; overflow -> grid
zstyle ':clicue:*' tier2-rows auto    # grid fills the terminal; or a fixed count
zstyle ':clicue:*' enabled   yes

# ── argument position: what you typed, explained ─────────────────────────────
# Past the command name, the second box stops browsing commands — that question
# is already answered — and enumerates the properties on the line instead:
#
#   ├ typed ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
#   │   -l                   long listing                                   │
#   │   -a, --all            list entries starting with .                    │
#   │   -t                   sort by modification time                      │
#   │   run 1×  ·  top 58% of your invocations  ·  today                    │
#
# Short and long spellings are paired by their shared description, which is how
# compsys happens to encode them. A clustered token like -lat is split only when
# EVERY letter is a flag the command documents — `ps -aux` is deliberately NOT
# explained, because ps does not document a dashed `u`.
#
# The flag set comes from compsys, which forks, so it arrives on the first Tab for
# a command and from ~/.cache/clicue/flags thereafter (keyed on the binary mtime).
# Descriptions lead; the usage count trails them rather than replacing them.

# ── verbosity for invocations you already know ───────────────────────────────
# 0 disables this entirely, which is the default: a card that quietly shows less
# than it did yesterday is indistinguishable from a broken one.
#
# Set it to a percentile (say 5) and any command+flags combination in the top 5%
# of your own invocations collapses to a single evidence line naming the expand
# key. `rm -rf` measured top 1% here, `ps -aux` top 7%, `ls -lat` top 58%.
zstyle ':clicue:*' familiar-percentile 0

# ── why these are fixed counts ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The card is always the same number of lines: tier1-rows + tier2-rows + 5.
# ZLE paints a taller POSTDISPLAY over a shorter one instead of reflowing, so a
# card that grew or shrank while typing would mangle itself. Both boxes pad to
# their allocation, and when there is no overflow the primary card absorbs the
# grid's border and rows so the total is identical either way.
#
# The primary card holds the top N ranked cues — history first, then everything
# else alphabetically. Anything past N flows into the grid.

# ── navigation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   Tab      cycle within the primary card. That card is history-ranked, so the
#            cue you want is usually one or two presses away. The ordering is
#            LEARNED rather than deterministic, and stabilises statistically as
#            history accumulates. Nothing is inserted — the proposal shows as
#            ghost text.
#   ↑ ↓      once you have started cycling, walk the selection. Off the end of
#            the primary card it continues straight into the grid.
#   ← →      jump a grid column. Meaningful only inside the grid, so Right keeps
#            working as the autosuggestion accept everywhere else.
#   Enter    put the highlighted cue on the command line — NOT run it. Choosing
#            from a card is composition, not execution; you still decide when to
#            run. Untouched, Enter executes exactly as always.
#   Esc      dismiss until the line is cleared
#
# Arrows and Enter are only taken WHILE a card is being navigated, and delegate
# to whatever owned them otherwise. Two bindings are refused outright and say so
# on stderr: unmodified Up/Down as a scroll key, and any bare printable
# character — binding 'q' as an alternate dismiss would break every command
# containing a q.

# ── keys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every binding is configuration. Multiple sequences per action are fine —
# terminals disagree about what they send.
#
# To discover what a key actually emits:   cat -v    then press it.
# If nothing prints, the TERMINAL consumed it and no shell config can help.
#
# Plain Up/Down are never bound by clicue — they stay on history-substring-search.

#   Shift+Arrow  — preferred, but konsole eats it (see below)
#   Alt+Arrow    — works today
#   Alt+J / Alt+K — home row, no reach; works today
zstyle ':clicue:keys' scroll-down '^[[1;2B' '^[[1;3B' '^[^[[B' '^[j'
zstyle ':clicue:keys' scroll-up   '^[[1;2A' '^[[1;3A' '^[^[[A' '^[k'
zstyle ':clicue:keys' accept      '^I'                           # Tab
zstyle ':clicue:keys' expand      '^[e'                    # Alt+E — open a
                                                                 # collapsed explanation
zstyle ':clicue:keys' dismiss     '^['                           # Esc (long reach —
                                                                 # change freely)

# Optional: force the hint-line label instead of deriving it.
# zstyle ':clicue:keys' scroll-label 'Shift+↑↓'

# ── Shift+Arrow and konsole ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# konsole binds Shift+Up/Down to Scroll Line Up/Down at the APPLICATION level,
# so those keystrokes never reach the shell. clicue detects konsole (via
# $KONSOLE_DBUS_SESSION) and advertises Alt+Arrow in the hint line instead.
# Both are bound, so the card works either way.
#
# The binding is NOT in Configure Keyboard Shortcuts. It lives in the profile's
# keyboard translator, compiled into libkonsoleprivate.so as:
#
#     key Up +Shift : scrollLineUp
#
# To free it:
#   Settings → Edit Current Profile → Keyboard tab
#   → Edit... (copies the built-in "default" translator)
#   → find the Shift+Up / Shift+Down entries bound to scrollLineUp/Down
#   → remove them, save under a new name, select it
#
# Until that is done, leave this alone: clicue auto-detects konsole and
# advertises Alt+↑↓ instead, which actually arrives. Uncomment only AFTER the
# translator is edited, or the card will advertise a key that cannot work.
#
# CLICUE_TERM_EATS_SHIFT=0

_clicue_src=$HOME/Projects/app/clicue/prototype/clicue.zsh
[[ -r $_clicue_src ]] && source $_clicue_src
unset _clicue_src
